## 6. Control logic updates (all live-verified over BACnet)
- **Heater**: ON when room temp falls below the heating setpoint (default 20.0 °C, adjustable over BACnet), off again at setpoint + 1.0 °C hysteresis. Relay on the spare input channel; status mirrored as a read-only BACnet object. Caught a real cold event during testing.
- **Alarm**: ON **only** above the alarm trip point (default 40.1 °C — the value from the earlier trip script). The AC trip never sounds the alarm (an earlier build had it tied to the AC trip; corrected per spec). Alarm relay as wired in the trip script; status mirrored read-only.
- **Fan**: ON when **neither** AC nor heater is calling, OFF otherwise — replaces the docx manual fan override; state mirrored to the fan object. All three states verified live.

## 7. BACnet object map (device 1234, "BMS_Controller_01")
| Object | Name | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| AI:1 | Room_Temperature | 1-Wire temperature sensor |
| AV:1 | Temperature_Setpoint | default 24.0 °C (AC on above) — adjustable over BACnet |
| AV:2 | Heating_Setpoint | default 20.0 °C — adjustable |
| AV:3 | Alarm_Trippoint | default 40.1 °C — adjustable |
| BO:1 | AC_Compressor_Blue | In1 |
| BO:2 | Blower_Fan_White | In2, mirrors automatic fan state |
| BO:3 | Heater_Status | In3 (added today) |
| BO:4 | Alarm_Status | In4 |
| AV:99 | honeypot | watch-object for intrusion practice |

Relays are **active-low** on this board. The hardware watchdog heartbeat is **enabled** (boot config overlay confirmed today) — stop the script with Ctrl-C / SIGINT, not SIGTERM, so relays turn off and the watchdog closes cleanly (otherwise the Pi hard-reboots in ~16 s).

## 8. Where things live
- The master script and self-test are **not published here** — they are built on the original builder's lab code and stay private.
- Live copies sit on the Pi in its home directory, executable, with the real LAN address supplied at runtime.
- Run on the Pi by invoking the scripts directly — their header line points at the correct virtual-environment interpreter.
  - A "no module named BAC0" error means the script was started with the system Python instead of the virtual environment's; run the script directly or activate the environment first.
